          </div>
        </figure>

        <p>The core judgment first: at 400 gsm, the factory matters less than the fabric supply chain behind it. A cut-and-sew operation with decent machines but no control over knitting will quote you a sharp price, then swap in a lighter, cheaper fleece once bulk starts. Your job during sourcing is to find out, before the deposit leaves your account, whether the mill behind the quote can hold 400 gsm across a 3,000-piece run.</p>

        <p>What follows is a 7-point vetting framework, the specific red flags that separate real heavyweight manufacturers from trading companies, and a scoring method you can apply to any shortlist. Build your own ranking from it. "Best" only means best for your order size, your fabric spec, and your delivery window.</p>

        <h2 id="why-harder">Why 400 GSM Hoodies Are Harder to Source Than You Think</h2>

        <p>In knitwear, 400 gsm sits firmly in the heavyweight bracket. Mass-market hoodies run 280–320 gsm. Cross roughly 380 gsm and the fabric gets denser, the knitting machine slows down, and the garment gains real weight. Every downstream variable shifts with it: yarn consumption per piece, loop-wheel and fleece knitting capacity, sewing machine tension settings, carton cube, freight cost.</p>

        <p>So 400 gsm works as a filter on your supplier list before it works as a line on your spec sheet. Plenty of suppliers will accept the inquiry. Far fewer hold the number.</p>

        <p>Four failure modes account for most of the complaints:</p>

        <ul class="ar-list"><li><strong>Weight substitution.</strong> The sample arrives at a true 400 gsm; bulk lands at 340–360 gsm. Visually similar, measurably lighter, and worth a meaningful margin per piece to the factory.</li><li><strong>Blend swapping.</strong> You specified 100% cotton. You receive a cotton-poly blend that pills differently and takes dye differently, which shows up after three or four home washes.</li><li><strong>"Heavyweight" as marketing language.</strong> Some suppliers tag anything above 300 gsm as heavyweight, so your 400 gsm request quietly gets quoted against a 320 gsm base cloth.</li><li><strong>Shrinkage masking.</strong> Fabric that weighs 400 gsm greige can drop below target after washing if it was never properly pre-shrunk or compacted. The weight you record on day one can differ from what your customer receives after the first wash.</li></ul>

        <p>None of this is exotic fraud. It is routine cost engineering, and it happens because the buyer never verified the fabric independently.</p>

        <h2 id="what-400-means">What "400 GSM" Actually Means (And How to Verify It)</h2>

        <p>GSM stands for grams per square meter: fabric weight per unit area. For knit fleece it tracks closely with thickness, warmth, opacity, and hand feel. A 400 gsm French terry hoodie feels substantial and holds its shape across the shoulders. Drop the same construction to 300 gsm and it reads thinner and drapes loose.</p>

        <p>Weight is not the same as quality. But at this threshold it is the number most likely to be fudged, so verify it before anything else.</p>

        <p><strong>How to verify fabric weight before committing:</strong></p>

        <ol class="ar-list"><li><strong>Weigh the sample yourself.</strong> Cut a precise 10 cm × 10 cm square from a flat, unstretched area, mid-back, away from seams and ribbing. Weigh it on a jeweler's scale accurate to 0.01 g and multiply by 100. A 10 × 10 cm piece at 400 gsm should land near 4.0 g.</li><li><strong>Request a lab test report.</strong> A third-party fabric weight test gives you a figure you can write into the purchase order and hold the supplier to.</li><li><strong>Test after washing.</strong> Wash and dry the swatch exactly as your published care label instructs, then re-weigh. Minor loss is normal. A steep drop points to inadequate compaction.</li><li><strong>Measure bulk against the approved sample.</strong> Pull the test garment from mid-run; the first carton is usually the most carefully made. Then repeat the swatch test.</li></ol>

        <p><strong>On tolerance:</strong> fabric weight never runs perfectly uniform across a roll, let alone across a production run. Agree a tolerance band around the target instead of demanding an exact figure, and put that band in the spec sheet and the PO. A mill that refuses to commit to a tolerance in writing has told you everything you need to know.</p>

