        <h3>1. Fabric &amp; Material Capability</h3>

        <p>One question carries most of the weight here: does the supplier knit its own 400 gsm fleece or source it from a mill it can actually name?</p>

        <div class="ar-table-wrap"><table class="ar-table"><thead><tr><th>Dimension</th><th>Qualified signal</th><th>Danger signal</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Fabric source</td><td>Named mill, verifiable, willing to share greige specs</td><td>Vague "our fabric is good quality" answers</td></tr><tr><td>Composition control</td><td>Written fiber content with tested percentages</td><td>Verbal "100% cotton" with no test report</td></tr><tr><td>Weight consistency</td><td>Committed tolerance band in writing</td><td>Refuses to state a tolerance</td></tr><tr><td>Fabric library</td><td>Multiple 400 gsm constructions on hand</td><td>One option, no swatch range</td></tr></tbody></table></div>

        <h3>2. Stitching, Construction &amp; Fit Accuracy</h3>

        <p>Heavyweight fleece is harder to sew than jersey. Thick seams, bulky hems, and dense loops demand the right needle size, correct thread tension, and a properly set-up machine. Ask for close-ups of cover-stitched hems, flatlock shoulder seams, and the hood-to-body join. Loose, wavy stitching on a 400 gsm sample means the operator is running light-fabric settings.</p>

        <p>Fit accuracy is the other half. Does the bulk garment match the approved sample's measurements within your tolerance? Request a measurement spec sheet and confirm exactly which points of measure get checked.</p>

        <h3>3. Sampling Accuracy: Sample vs. Bulk Consistency</h3>

        <p>The sample is a promise. Bulk is delivery. Most sourcing relationships break in the gap between the two.</p>

        <p>Ask it plainly: will bulk run on the same fabric lot as the sample, or a new lot? If it is a new lot, ask how weight and shade get matched. A supplier with real sampling discipline will describe its approved-sample procedure without prompting, including where the golden sample is stored and how it gets referenced on the line. Our <a href="/production/">hoodie sampling process</a> walks through a controlled sample-to-bulk handoff step by step.</p>

        <h3>4. Quality Control &amp; Inspection Process</h3>

        <p>Three questions: what gets checked, at which stage, and by whom. A credible answer includes inline inspection during sewing as well as a final random check before packing. Look for AQL-based inspection documentation, measurement checks, and a written defect classification. Vague answers here mean the QC is visual and informal, whatever the website claims. See <a href="/our-services/">factory audit and QC standards</a> for what a structured audit actually covers.</p>

        <h3>5. Capacity, Lead Time &amp; MOQ Flexibility</h3>

        <p>Capacity has to fit your order size in both directions. A factory optimized for 50,000-unit programs will push your 800-piece order to the back of the queue, and a 30-machine workshop rarely survives a sudden 10x scale-up. Ask for monthly capacity on heavyweight fleece specifically; headline capacity across every category a supplier lists tells you little about its fleece lines.</p>

        <p>MOQ: for custom 400 gsm hoodies with your own labels and trims, expect 300–500 pieces per color. Custom-dyed fleece pushes that higher, since the dye house has its own minimums. Confirm the number per color, not per order.</p>

        <h3>6. Compliance, Certifications &amp; Testing Reports</h3>

        <p>Ask which certifications the factory holds and whether they are current. Apparel programs typically cover social compliance, quality management systems, and environmental standards. A certificate image proves nothing. Verify the certificate number with the issuing body, check the expiry date, and read the scope carefully; a scope often names one production site while the goods get made at a sister facility across town.</p>

        <p>Testing reports deserve the same scrutiny. Fiber composition, colorfastness, dimensional stability, pilling. Request reports for the exact fabric you are buying, and check that the report references the same construction and composition as your spec sheet.</p>

        <h3>7. Communication &amp; Responsiveness</h3>

        <p>Underrated, and highly predictive. A supplier who answers technical questions precisely, raises problems before you notice them, and gives you one named contact will behave the same way when a knitting lot comes in short. One who answers vaguely during the sales phase will go quiet in a crisis. Send a specific technical question before you commit. The quality of the answer tells you more than how fast it arrives.</p>

        <h2 id="red-flags">Red Flags: 6 Common Mistakes Buyers Make When Sourcing 400 GSM Hoodies</h2>

        <p><strong>1. Choosing on price alone.</strong> The lowest quote almost always hides a lighter fabric, a cheaper blend, or a margin too thin to survive the order. Compare quotes against one fixed spec sheet so every number describes the same garment.</p>

        <p><strong>2. Skipping independent fabric verification.</strong> Weigh the swatch yourself or pay for a lab test. That single step prevents the most common and most expensive substitution in this category.</p>

        <p><strong>3. Assuming the sample equals the bulk.</strong> Approve the sample in writing, keep a sealed golden sample, and inspect bulk against it. A photo thread is not an approved sample.</p>

        <p><strong>4. Accepting certifications at face value.</strong> Check numbers, expiry dates, and scope. A certificate issued to a different facility carries no weight on your order.</p>

        <p><strong>5. Ignoring MOQ structure.</strong> MOQ per color and MOQ per fabric can diverge sharply from the order-level minimum a supplier quotes first. Settle this before you lock your colorway range.</p>

        <p><strong>6. Skipping the factory visit or third-party audit.</strong> Can't travel? Commission a third-party inspection. An audit before deposit costs a fraction of a failed bulk run.</p>

